Package: unim-common
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         dbus
Recommends: im-config, fonts-noto-cjk,
            pulseaudio-utils | pipewire-bin | alsa-utils
Description: UNIM Korean IME — core engine, daemon, CLI, shared library
 UNIM is a modular Korean Input Method Engine written in Rust with
 2-bul and 3-bul Hangul layouts and automatic Korean/English typo
 correction (TypeFix).
 .
 This package contains the pieces every UNIM install needs:
  * libunim_capi shared library (stable internal C-API consumed by
    GTK/Qt input method modules)
  * unim-daemon — central engine, D-Bus session-activated
  * unim-cli — unified command-line interface (incl. `config`
    subcommand)
  * D-Bus service file, icons, im-config integration, man pages
 .
 Install a frontend package (unim-xim, unim-wayland) and an IM module
 package (unim-im-gtk, unim-im-qt) to actually type Korean in apps,
 or install the `unim` meta-package for the full stack.

Package: unim-im-gtk
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: unim-xim | unim-wayland
Description: UNIM Korean IME — GTK3/GTK4 input method modules
 GTK3 and GTK4 input method modules for the UNIM Korean Input Method
 Engine. Enables Korean typing in every GTK-based application
 (GNOME, Xfce, MATE, Cinnamon, GIMP, Inkscape, etc.).
 .
 The modules delegate all composition work to unim-daemon over D-Bus
 and ship as standard GTK immodules discovered via the multiarch
 immodules directory.

Package: unim-im-qt
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: unim-xim | unim-wayland
Description: UNIM Korean IME — Qt5/Qt6 platform input context plugins
 Qt5 and Qt6 platform input context plugins for the UNIM Korean
 Input Method Engine. Enables Korean typing in every Qt-based
 application (KDE Plasma apps, Telegram Desktop, qBittorrent, Qt
 Creator, many cross-platform editors).
 .
 The plugins delegate all composition work to unim-daemon over D-Bus
 and ship under Qt's platforminputcontexts plugin directory.

Package: unim-xim
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: UNIM Korean IME — XIM protocol frontend (X11)
 X11 XIM protocol server for the UNIM Korean Input Method Engine.
 Required in X11 sessions and for legacy applications that only
 speak XIM (e.g. xterm, emacs -nw, many Java/AWT apps).
 .
 Install this package alongside the GTK/Qt IM module packages when
 running an X11 session. On Wayland sessions, install unim-wayland
 instead (both may coexist for XWayland fallback).

Package: unim-wayland
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: UNIM Korean IME — Wayland input-method frontend
 Wayland input-method-v2 + virtual-keyboard-v1 protocol server for
 the UNIM Korean Input Method Engine, with hanja/special-character
 popup via zwp_input_popup_surface_v2.
 .
 Required in pure Wayland sessions (KDE Plasma Wayland, Sway,
 Hyprland, COSMIC, Labwc, Wayfire). GNOME Shell users should
 additionally install unim-gnome for native indicator integration.

Package: unim-desktop
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: unim-im-gtk
Replaces: unim-gui-qt (<< 0.3.0~),
          unim-indicator (<< 0.3.0-1.1~),
          unim-settings (<< 0.3.0-1.1~),
          unim-popup-service (<< 0.3.0-1.1~)
Breaks: unim-gui-qt (<< 0.3.0~),
        unim-indicator (<< 0.3.0-1.1~),
        unim-settings (<< 0.3.0-1.1~),
        unim-popup-service (<< 0.3.0-1.1~)
Description: UNIM Korean IME — desktop UI (tray indicator, settings, popups)
 GTK4 desktop user-interface bundle for the UNIM Korean Input Method
 Engine on non-GNOME desktops (KDE Plasma, Xfce, Cinnamon, MATE).
 Bundles the three UI processes that are always deployed together:
  * tray indicator (StatusNotifierItem) — global mode toggle and menu
  * settings dialog (unim-settings-gtk, Adw.PreferencesWindow) — hotkeys,
    layouts, TypeFix, blacklist, popup behaviour
  * popup & tray service — hanja/special-character/emoji popups,
    D-Bus-activated, with X11 (override-redirect) and Wayland
    (xdg-popup / input_popup_v2) backends
 .
 GNOME Shell users get the same functionality natively via the
 "unim-gnome" extension and normally do not need this package.
 .
 Replaces the previous unim-gui-qt binary; the Qt6/QML GUI has been
 deprecated and removed in favour of a single GTK4 codebase. This
 package supersedes the earlier split unim-indicator, unim-settings
 (the pre-0.3.63 GTK dialog, now shipped here as unim-settings-gtk —
 not the new Slint unim-settings package) and unim-popup-service
 packages.

Package: unim-settings
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: im-config, fontconfig
Replaces: unim-desktop (<< 0.3.63-1~)
Breaks: unim-desktop (<< 0.3.63-1~)
Description: UNIM Korean IME — settings app & first-run wizard (Slint)
 Cross-platform settings application (Slint, winit + Skia) sharing one
 codebase with the Windows build. Includes the first-run wizard
 (--first-run, --whats-new) and its per-login autostart gate
 (--first-run-if-needed) that guides default-IME setup via im-config.
 .
 Takes over /usr/bin/unim-settings from earlier unim-desktop builds,
 where that name was the GTK4 dialog (now unim-settings-gtk).

Package: unim-keymap-studio
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: UNIM Keymap Studio — view and edit Hangul keymaps
 GTK4/libadwaita tool that opens any v1·v2·v3 Hangul keymap
 profile (built-in or user-defined), shows the 4×N key layout
 with Upper/Lower toggle and per-key metadata, and lets you edit
 individual keys, cho/jung/jong combinations, rule sets and the
 moachigi marker. Saves to ~/.config/unim/layouts/ so the result
 is immediately picked up by the rest of UNIM.

Package: unim-typing-practice
Architecture: any
Depends: unim-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: UNIM Typing Practice — WPM/accuracy/heatmap on any Hangul keymap
 GTK4/libadwaita tool that runs a typing-practice session on
 the currently selected UNIM Hangul keymap. Captures raw ASCII
 keys (bypassing any active system IME), feeds them through the
 keymap's composer, and reports live WPM, CPM, accuracy and
 error rate. After each session the per-key error heatmap is
 painted on top of the keyboard.

Package: unim-gnome
Architecture: all
Depends: unim-common (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-desktop (= ${source:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends},
         gnome-shell
Description: UNIM Korean IME — GNOME Shell extension
 Native GNOME Shell extension for the UNIM Korean Input Method
 Engine. Adds a top-panel indicator, hotkey management, per-app
 input-mode rules, and virtual keyboard / preedit overlay on
 Wayland (Mutter).
 .
 Hanja / special-character / emoji popups and the settings dialog
 come from the companion `unim-desktop` bundle (GTK4), shared across
 all desktops. On GNOME its tray indicator stays dormant — this
 extension provides the panel indicator natively.

Package: unim
Architecture: all
Depends: unim-common (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-im-gtk (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-im-qt (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-xim (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-wayland (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-desktop (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-settings (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-keymap-studio (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-typing-practice (= ${source:Version}),
         unim-gnome (= ${source:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Korean Input Method Engine — full stack (meta-package)
 Meta-package that pulls in the complete UNIM Korean Input Method
 Engine: core engine, every IM module, every protocol frontend,
 both GUIs, and the GNOME Shell extension. Install this when you
 want "UNIM, everything included" without picking individual
 packages.
 .
 Power users who want a smaller footprint can install only the
 pieces they need — see the individual package descriptions.
